First Harbor Bank is filing this SAR on customer Daniel Mercer, age 38, of Tacoma, Washington, regarding suspected structuring and possible laundering of fraud proceeds. Mercer opened personal checking account 44198217 on January 12, 2026, and stated his occupation as a self-employed residential contractor with expected monthly account activity of approximately $8,000 to $12,000.

Between June 3, 2026, and July 18, 2026, Mercer made 14 cash deposits totaling $126,450 at First Harbor Bank branches in Tacoma, Lakewood, and Federal Way, Washington. Twelve of the deposits ranged from $8,200 to $9,900. On four occasions, Mercer made deposits at two different branches on the same day. Bank records showed no comparable cash-deposit activity during the prior five months.

Within one to three business days of the deposits, Mercer transferred $109,800 to three newly added external accounts held by Mercer Holdings LLC at Pacific Commerce Bank. Public business records show Mercer formed Mercer Holdings LLC on May 27, 2026, approximately one week before the cash activity began. The company lists Mercer as its sole member and uses his residential address. The customer did not previously disclose the business relationship to First Harbor Bank.

On July 10, 2026, a branch employee asked Mercer about the source of a $9,700 cash deposit. Mercer stated the funds were payments from residential remodeling customers but declined to provide invoices or other supporting documentation. Review of account activity identified no payroll, building-supply purchases, contractor payments, tax payments, or other transactions consistent with the stated volume of contracting activity.

First Harbor Bank considers the activity suspicious because the repeated cash deposits below $10,000, use of multiple branches, rapid movement of funds to newly established business accounts, and activity inconsistent with the customer’s prior account history and stated business profile may indicate structuring and laundering of potentially illicit proceeds.

First Harbor Bank restricted outbound transfers from the account on July 19, 2026, pending enhanced review. The account remains open as of the date of this filing. Supporting account statements, deposit records, branch surveillance records, and customer due-diligence information are available upon request.
